\title{MO-1: the reference engine in the default configuration, a census, and the switch-off of the re-check}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{reference-engine}
\tag{soundness}
\tag{driver}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Package "MO-1", 2026-11-25. The maintainer's instruction of that day, verbatim: "turn off reference engine recheck on default. try make machine align with official behavior. reference engine is just a fallback that is used to verify the behavior during development. It shall not affect real production." The target is a default configuration in which the machine is the whole checker and gives the official verdict by itself. This report has two parts. Part A is the census: on every input set of the project, where the default configuration of the published build uses [the reference engine](design-reference-engine) today, and what the machine answers without it. Part B is a patch, not applied and not published, that turns the re-check of a rejection and the launcher's strict re-run off by default and keeps them as one development option. The decline fallback is not turned off; the option that would is in the patch, and what it gives is counted.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The default configuration uses the reference engine in four places, not three.} (1) Every rejection of the machine is re-checked: a strict machine, then the reference engine, see [the verdict protocol](design-verdict-protocol-recheck). (2) Every unit the machine declines is tried again cold and then decided by the reference engine. (3) The launcher checks a rejected file with a dropped metadata node again under the strict policy, see [the decision](dec-7UAL). (4) The environment pass admits every inductive block and the quotient package with judgments of the reference engine, in every configuration: 609 units of Init and 7,133 of Mathlib (section 2.4). The fourth was not part of the task and is not touched; it is counted here because the target cannot be reached without it.}
\li{\strong{(1), the re-check, changes no verdict on real input.} On the eight corpora the machine rejects nothing. On the 262 small arena tests it rejects 54 units and on the 17,775 mutants 1,906, and the re-check turns none of these 1,960 rejections into anything else. Only on the hostile suite does the re-check change anything: of 737 rejections it turns 16 into accepts, 12 through the strict machine and 4 through the reference engine. 12 of the 16 are accepts of the official checker, so without the re-check the default configuration has \strong{12 known wrong rejects}; the other 4 are rejections of the official checker that the re-check had turned into tolerated accepts, and without it they get the official verdict.}
\li{\strong{(3), the strict re-run, never fires on a hostile row, an arena test or a corpus.} With the machine as default engine it fires on 485 mutants and changes 351 verdicts, each from a correct rejection to a decline. The four metadata files of [finding R2-F4](ex-FD6K), for which it was built when the reference engine was the default engine, are accepted by the machine itself. Without the net the mutant gate goes from 15,741 in agreement and 2,034 declined to 16,092 and 1,683, with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0 as before.}
\li{\strong{(2), the fallback, is what real input needs.} The machine declines 95 units of the eight corpora, 80 different declarations: 86 by the rule of finding E1-F6 (the K rule after an applied let variable) and 9 because a probe of eager reduction was cut. Init and reachability have none; Std 1, cedar 1, con-leche 2, navier-stokes-euler 16, cslib 11, Mathlib 64. The machine alone therefore declines six of the eight corpora at their first such unit. On the small inputs it declines 8 more units that matter: 4 hostile rows, the 2 arena tests on which the official kernel does not terminate, and 2 mutants for memory.}
\li{\strong{The machine alone gives no wrong accept that the default configuration does not give.} Turning any of the three off can only turn an accept into a rejection or a decline. Measured, file by file, against the official verdicts: no accept of a statement that is not derivable on any input set, in any of the three configurations.}
\li{\strong{The tolerated accepts.} 78 hostile rows are accepted against the official rejection today, 74 without the re-check. By what produces them (section 5.3): 45 need the shortcut \code{congruence} or \code{skipargs}, 22 of those under eager reduction; 15 need a memo that survives a declaration (\code{warm}, \code{pchk}); 1 the tabling gate; 13 are accepted by the machine without any switch, 4 of them by the reference engine too. Aligning the first group costs what the two shortcuts gain: without them the geometric mean of the eight warm passes was 15 \% longer and Mathlib 42 \% longer when the default set was chosen. The second costs what warm sessions gain, between nothing and 5 \% of one worker's CPU time where it was measured.}
\li{\strong{The schedule.} A rejection or an accept that rests on a memo of an earlier declaration depends on which session checked that declaration. The driver's own invariance suite, with the machine as primary engine on the hostile suite, finds 12 rows whose verdict differs between its nine schedules \em{with} the re-check, and 21 without it. On the published tests and the mutant corpora it finds none, without the re-check: 17,159 files (section 5.4, and [the insight](ins-RPOG)).}
\li{\strong{The patch.} Five diffs: the driver's default, one launcher option \code{\verb~|-X verify=none|reference~}, the gate scripts, one line of the machine's gate, twelve marks in the hostile manifest. A plain run says \code{\verb~|verify=none fallback=reference~}; \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} is the configuration that was the default; \code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~} is the machine alone. The gates of the patched build: the suites of the three modules; the gate of the default configuration through the executable with 230 of 230 tests, 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0 (16,092 in agreement, 1,683 declined) and on the hostile suite 1,184 in agreement, 74 tolerated, 12 known wrong rejects, 4 declined, no wrong accept and no other wrong reject; the same gate with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} gives the published counts, 15,741 and 2,034, 1,192 and 78 and 4; the gate of the default configuration through the jars on the JVM has the counts of the executable; the eight corpora accepted with 8 and 4 workers on both launchers; every one of 19,319 files gets, under each of the three configurations, the verdict that the census of the published build predicted for it.}
\li{\strong{Verdict.} The switch-off of (1) and (3) costs nothing on anything real and 12 wrong rejects on hand-made files, all of two known classes. It contradicts one sentence of [the standing policies](standing-policies-exactness), "never a rejection of what the official checker accepts ... also on a hand-made file", which the maintainer's instruction supersedes for the time until the machine is aligned; the gate records the twelve by name with a count that may only go down. (2) cannot go yet: Mathlib, cslib, navier-stokes-euler, con-leche, cedar and Std would be declined.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. Conditions}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The published build}: the shared distribution of 2026-11-25 20:00 UTC, jars sha256 \code{f5517aa135d2}, the build of [the integration of the second wave](rep-INT9); a private byte-identical copy of it. Everything in sections 2 to 5 is from its executable (GraalVM Community Edition native image) unless a line says otherwise.}
\li{\strong{The configurations.} "Old default": a run without any \code{-X} option, which re-checks, falls back and has the net. "New default": \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck~}. "Machine alone": \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck -X no-fallback~}. The published build has no option that turns the net off, and the net also fires in the last two; where it fired, the statistics line names it, the verdict of the practical run was a rejection by definition, and that rejection is the verdict counted for the configuration. The patched build of section 7 has the three as \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}, no option, and \code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~}; every file was run again on it (section 8).}
\li{\strong{The input sets}, with their oracles. The eight corpora. The 270 tests of the arena, 148 good, 92 bad and 30 of the class \code{either}; the oracle is the stored verdict of the official checker of Lean 4.36.0 for 260 of them and the arena's class for the corpora and two large good tests. The four mutant corpora, 17,775 files with stored official verdicts (12,260 tutorial, 2,692 bugs, 1,976 metadata, 847 prelude). The hostile suite, 1,274 rows with official verdicts and marks, see [the gate](design-gate-hostile). 19,319 files in all, the eight corpora counted once.}
\li{\strong{A run} is the default configuration as a user runs it: automatic workers (8 on the corpora, one per 128 units on small files), the default load mode, the default switch set. The mutants and the hostile rows ran with the heap cap of 4 GB that \src{scripts/gate.sh} gives them. All runs were made on the 10 cores outside the measurement set, four at a time for the small files, one at a time for the corpora. \strong{Nothing in this report is a timing.}}
\li{\strong{What was read per run}: the exit status; the checker's statistics line; the statistics file (\code{\verb~|-X stats-file~}): the driver's counts and events and the counts of the machine, of the strict machine and of the reference engine; and the machine's log of every outcome that is not an accept (\code{\verb~|-X machine.declines~}: outcome, error code, unit, declaration, message). A unit "the machine declines" is a unit whose warm attempt ends in a decline; a decline of the strict machine behind a warm rejection is a stage of the re-check and is not counted as one.}
\li{\strong{The switch ablation} of sections 5.2 and 5.3 ran the rows in question with the default set less one switch (\code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=default,-NAME~}), with no switch, and with the machine's session forgotten before every declaration (\code{\verb~|-X machine.session-kib=1~}).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The units the machine declines}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 The eight corpora}

\p{Old default, 8 workers. "Environment pass" are the units decided by admission with the reference engine (section 2.4). "Repeats" are admitted without a check on the word of their original, see [the driver](design-driver-repeats); their number moves by a few units between runs with more than one worker. The machine rejects no unit of any corpus, so the re-check never runs there, and no corpus has a metadata record.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{Units}\th{Environment pass}\th{Repeats}\th{Checked by the machine}\th{Rejected by it}\th{Declined by it, decided by the reference engine}\th{of them E1-F6}\th{of them a cut probe}\th{The machine alone}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{58,547}\td{609}\td{5,826}\td{52,112}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{accept}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{101,146}\td{1,050}\td{8,518}\td{91,578}\td{0}\td{1}\td{1}\td{0}\td{decline at unit 20,634}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{146,058}\td{1,960}\td{15,137}\td{128,961}\td{0}\td{1}\td{1}\td{0}\td{decline at unit 36,506}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{4,413}\td{189}\td{199}\td{4,025}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{accept}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{36,747}\td{473}\td{4,271}\td{32,003}\td{0}\td{2}\td{1}\td{1}\td{decline at unit 13,027}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{126,468}\td{1,618}\td{13,014}\td{111,836}\td{0}\td{16}\td{16}\td{0}\td{decline at unit 38,006}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{401,985}\td{4,377}\td{30,209}\td{367,399}\td{0}\td{11}\td{7}\td{4}\td{decline at unit 71,375}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{721,845}\td{7,133}\td{59,434}\td{655,278}\td{0}\td{64}\td{60}\td{4}\td{decline at unit 34,631}}
\tr{\td{\strong{sum}}\td{1,597,209}\td{17,409}\td{136,608}\td{1,443,192}\td{0}\td{\strong{95}}\td{86}\td{9}\td{2 accept, 6 decline}}
}

\p{The reference engine accepts all 95; the counts 0, 1, 1, 0, 2, 16, 11, 64 are the ones known before. Every one of the units is declined twice by the machine, warm and in its cold retry, with the same reason. The 95 units are 80 different declarations, because the corpora share libraries: 75 of class E1-F6 and 5 of the cut probes.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{E1-F6}, error code \code{E_LIMIT}, text "K rule after the application of a let variable whose declared type is not the type of its value (finding E1-F6): the type of the major premise may differ from the official one; the reference engine checks this declaration". Examples: \code{\verb~|Std.IterM.Equiv.lift_step_bind_congr~}, the one unit of Std and of cedar, also in cslib and Mathlib; \code{\verb~|ConLeche.Model.harvestDefn~} on con-leche; \code{\verb~|FormalMultilinearSeries.comp_assoc~}, the first of navier-stokes-euler and of cslib, also in Mathlib; \code{\verb~|LinearMap.exists_basis_basis_of_span_eq_top_of_mem_algebraMap~}, the first of Mathlib. Of the 16 of navier-stokes-euler, 12 are declarations of the project itself and 4 of Mathlib. Of Mathlib's 60, 17 are in \code{CategoryTheory} and 7 are private auxiliary declarations; the rest are spread over analysis, measure and probability theory, algebraic geometry and linear algebra.}
\li{\strong{A cut probe}, error code \code{E_LIMIT}, text "eager reduction, and a probe of the PLAIN phase (the test 'X = Bool.true' or a Nat folding on an open term, which only eager reduction evaluates) was not decided within its budget (-X machine.eager-probe)". Five declarations: \code{\verb~|ConLeche.lpOfData_pack~} on con-leche, and four private proofs of \code{\verb~|Batteries.Data.Char.Basic~} (of \code{Char.all}, \code{Char.any} and two of \code{\verb~|Char.of_any_eq_false_aux~}) on cslib and on Mathlib. These are the five units of [the probe budget](design-switches).}
\li{\strong{No unit} of a corpus was declined for a tick bound or for memory in these runs.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.2 The small inputs}

\table{
\tr{\th{Input set}\th{Files}\th{Units the machine declines}\th{Class}\th{What the reference engine answers}}
\tr{\td{arena, 262 small tests}\td{262}\td{2 in 2 files}\td{the machine's bound of 2^27 ticks per unit: \code{\verb~|corner-cases/eta-struct-major-no-whnf~}, \code{\verb~|corner-cases/eta-struct-major-and-no-whnf~}}\td{declines too: the reduction returns to a term it started from, the official kernel does not terminate. Both tests are of the class \code{either}.}}
\tr{\td{hostile suite}\td{1,274}\td{4 in 4 files}\td{3 of E1-F6: \code{\verb~|e1-sys-let-krule-major-applied-bad~}, \code{\verb~|e1-sys-let-krule-major-applied-ok~}, \code{\verb~|e1-sib-krule-major-proj-applied-let-bad~}. 1 of eager reduction, "eager reduction lifts a hasFVar guard (Nat folding) INSIDE the comparison of the K rule": \code{\verb~|e1-krule-benign-eager-alone-ok~}}\td{the official verdict in all four: reject, accept, reject; accept}}
\tr{\td{mutants, bugs}\td{2,692}\td{2 in 2 files}\td{memory, \code{BUDGET_MEMORY}: the \code{\verb~|change_literal~} mutants \code{s1.0000} of \code{\verb~|bugs/proj-of-subst-prop~} and of \code{\verb~|bugs/rec-of-subst-prop~}. The machine alone also declines them with the executable's own heap of 13 GB, after a minute and 7.8 GB resident.}\td{rejects both, as the official checker does}}
\tr{\td{mutants, prelude}\td{847}\td{13 in 7 files}\td{\code{INTERNAL_ERROR}: units that lie above the deciding unit of a file whose earlier declaration was rejected for a loose bound variable; with 8 workers they are started before the rejection is recorded}\td{no verdict depends on them}}
\tr{\td{mutants, tutorial and metadata}\td{14,236}\td{0}\td{}\td{}}
}

\p{The four declines of the hostile suite that every configuration has are not the machine's: one by admission (a string literal without its prerequisites) and three by the loader (a metadata node around \code{eagerReduce}, and inside an inductive record twice). The 1,683 declined mutants of the new default are likewise declines of the loader and of admission, the ones the reference pipeline has.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.3 The classes, and what the machine would have to do}

\table{
\tr{\th{Class}\th{Units on real input}\th{Files}\th{What the machine would have to do to decide it officially}}
\tr{\td{E1-F6: the K rule after the application of a let variable whose declared type is not the type of its value}\td{86 of the corpora, 75 declarations}\td{6 corpora; 3 hostile rows}\td{Type the major premise of the K rule as the official kernel does, from the unreduced term: the machine's value of such an application has the let variable reduced away, so the type of the value can be a stand-in that is only convertible with the official one, see [class 1](design-official-divergence-standin). The value has to keep the let variable, or its declared type, to where the K rule asks. A second package works on this.}}
\tr{\td{A probe of the plain phase of eager reduction cut at its budget}\td{9 of the corpora, 5 declarations}\td{3 corpora}\td{Decide the probe: learn whether the plain and the eager mode agree at that point without evaluating what the official plain procedure never starts. The root cause of the large probe of con-leche is open, see [what the release leaves open](prob-HC9J). A third package works on this.}}
\tr{\td{Eager reduction inside the comparison of the K rule lifting a guard}\td{0}\td{1 hostile row}\td{The same question for the K rule: the result of a reduction would depend on the mode. Of the 20 rows with the note \code{eager-hard}, which the switch \code{eager} alone declines by design, the default set declines only this one; it accepts 10 official accepts by itself and 9 official rejections through the shortcut \code{congruence} (section 5.3).}}
\tr{\td{Memory}\td{0}\td{2 mutants}\td{Reject before the evaluation that needs more than the memory limit: the reference engine rejects both files within the limit, so the machine does work here that the reference engine does not. Not analysed further.}}
\tr{\td{The tick bound per unit}\td{0}\td{2 arena tests of the class \code{either}}\td{Nothing: the official kernel does not terminate, and a decline is the answer of both engines.}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.4 The fourth use: the environment pass}

\p{Inductive blocks and the quotient package are not checked by the machine in any configuration. [Admission](design-inductive-admission) regenerates and compares them with judgments of the reference engine, on the environment thread, and the driver records its outcome without confirmation because "the environment pass is the reference kernel". The column "Environment pass" of section 2.1 is that work: 17,409 units of the eight corpora, 1.1 \% of all units, 7,133 of Mathlib. Neither \code{\verb~|-X no-fallback~} nor anything in the patch changes it; "the machine alone" in this report means alone for definitions, theorems, axioms and opaques. A machine that is the whole checker needs admission to ask its judgments of the machine; admission asks them through one interface, which is where that would be done. How much the answers of the two engines can differ there was not examined.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The rejections of the machine, and what the re-check does with them}

\p{Old default. "Rejected by the machine" counts units whose warm attempt ends in a rejection; the strict machine and the reference engine then run on each.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Input set}\th{Files}\th{Units rejected by the machine}\th{in files}\th{Accepted by the strict machine (WARM-ANOMALY)}\th{Accepted by the reference engine (MACHINE-INCOMPLETE)}\th{Not confirmed: the reference engine declines}\th{Files whose verdict differs without the re-check}}
\tr{\td{eight corpora}\td{8}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{arena, small tests}\td{262}\td{54}\td{54}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{mutants, tutorial}\td{12,260}\td{1,185}\td{1,185}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{mutants, bugs}\td{2,692}\td{425}\td{421}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{mutants, metadata}\td{1,976}\td{158}\td{158}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{mutants, prelude}\td{847}\td{138}\td{111}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{hostile suite}\td{1,274}\td{737}\td{737}\td{12}\td{4}\td{0}\td{16}}
\tr{\td{\strong{sum}}\td{19,319}\td{2,697}\td{2,666}\td{12}\td{4}\td{0}\td{16}}
}

\p{Every other rejection was confirmed by the reference engine or abandoned because the unit lay above an earlier failure of its file (a run with several workers rejects more than one unit of some mutants). On the corpora, the arena tests and the mutants the re-check ran on 1,960 rejections of the machine and changed none. The 16 hostile rows, with the official verdict:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Row}\th{Official}\th{Old default}\th{New default}\th{Who accepts in the re-check}\th{The switch that makes the machine reject}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|s3-wc-proj-two-decls-ok~}, \code{\verb~|s3-wc-warm-samehead-two-decls-ok~}, \code{\verb~|r2-ptr-alone-ok~}, \code{\verb~|r2-ptr-other-record-order-ok~}, \code{\verb~|r2-ptr-same-record-ok~}, \code{\verb~|r2-ptr-same-record-def-ok~}, \code{\verb~|oh-kept-false-reused-ok~}, \code{\verb~|oh-kept-false-then-true-ok~}}\td{accept}\td{accept}\td{\strong{reject, wrong}}\td{the strict machine}\td{\code{warm}: without it, or with the session forgotten before each declaration, the machine accepts}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|s3-wc-canon-fvar-full-first-ok~}, \code{\verb~|r2-id-binder-name-ok~}, \code{\verb~|r2-id-binder-name-def-ok~}, \code{\verb~|r2-id-binder-info-ok~}}\td{accept}\td{accept}\td{\strong{reject, wrong}}\td{the reference engine only}\td{none: the machine rejects with no switch too}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|r2-ptr-other-record-bad~}, \code{\verb~|r2-ptr-other-record-def-bad~}}\td{reject}\td{accept, tolerated}\td{reject, official}\td{the strict machine}\td{\code{warm}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|s3-wc-inner-caseson-bad~}, \code{\verb~|s3-wc-inner-matcher-bad~}}\td{reject}\td{accept, tolerated}\td{reject, official}\td{the strict machine}\td{\code{casetrees}}}
}

\p{So the re-check repairs 12 wrong rejects and makes 4 tolerated accepts. A re-check by the strict machine alone, without the reference engine, would repair 8 of the 12; it was not built, because the instruction as read for this work is a rejection that is final. The 13th row with the note \code{recheck} in the manifest, \code{\verb~|s3-k2-krule-subst-hot-ok~}, is rejected only with the switch \code{k2}, which is in no default set.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The safety net}

\table{
\tr{\th{Input set}\th{Files on which the net fires}\th{What it changes}}
\tr{\td{eight corpora, 270 arena tests}\td{0}\td{nothing: no file has a metadata record}}
\tr{\td{hostile suite}\td{0}\td{nothing. 10 rows carry a metadata record and no practical run rejects one of them: 7 are accepted (6 that the official checker accepts and one tolerated accept) and 3 are declined by the loader. The four accepted files of finding R2-F4 (\code{\verb~|r2-mdata-beta-hidden-ok~}, \code{\verb~|-hidden-def-ok~}, \code{\verb~|-arg-hidden-ok~}, \code{\verb~|-bvar-hidden-ok~}) are accepted by the machine in all three configurations; the net was needed for them while the reference engine was the default engine, which rejects them under the practical policy.}}
