}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Part B: the switch-off of the re-check and of the safety net, as a patch}

\p{The patch is five files of unified diffs against the main tree of 2026-11-25 (the tree the published build was made from). It is not applied and not published. What it does, by module:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Patch}\th{What changes}}
\tr{\td{1, \code{rlvm-driver}}\td{\code{\verb~|RunConfig.recheckRejections~} is \code{false} by default: a rejection of the primary engine, warm or in its own cold retry of a decline, is recorded as it is; no strict run, no reference engine, no event. The driver gets two settings by name, \code{\verb~|verify=none|reference~} for that field and \code{\verb~|fallback=reference|none~} for \code{\verb~|RunConfig.referenceFallback~} (the existing \code{no-fallback} stays), and writes \code{driver.verify} and \code{driver.fallback} into the statistics file. No line of the protocol itself changed: the code path of a final rejection existed, as \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck~}. The suites of the driver ask for the verification by name in one place, their fixture; the enumeration of the protocol table starts from the default; four tests are new (the rejection out of a warm session that is final and depends on the session cap, the primary engine alone, the two settings by name, the invariance of the verdict under the default protocol for engines whose rejections do not depend on the session) and the test of a final rejection now also runs a configuration that says nothing. The invariance script and the driver's bench mirror the launcher.}}
\tr{\td{2, \code{rlvm-main}}\td{One option, \code{\verb~|-X verify=none|reference~}. \code{none}: the driver's default, and the launcher's safety net is off. \code{reference}: the re-check and the net, as before. Not given: \code{none}, unless the reference engine is the primary engine of the run (\code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~}, \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~}) or its oracle (\code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~}); those runs verify unasked and are what they were. \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck~} is \code{\verb~|verify=none~}; with \code{\verb~|verify=reference~} it is a usage error, as is \code{no-fallback} beside \code{\verb~|fallback=...~}. The line \code{default:} of \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~}, the help text and the statistics line say what is on.}}
\tr{\td{3, \code{scripts}}\td{\src{scripts/gate-hostile.py} knows the mark \code{wrong-reject} and a ceiling; \src{scripts/gate.sh} carries the ceiling as a constant, passes it, prints every known wrong reject by name on every run, and says in its header which configuration a gate means.}}
\tr{\td{4, the gate of the machine}\td{One line of the test harness of \code{rlvm-machine} (\code{M8Pipeline.machine}): the pipeline step of the machine's gate and the mutant campaign of the machine run with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}, because they read the counters that only the re-check fills and compare the final verdict with the reference pipeline's. No class of the machine itself is touched.}}
\tr{\td{5, the hostile manifest}\td{The mark \code{wrong-reject} on the twelve rows of section 5.2, beside the note \code{recheck} they have, each with its cause in the text of the row; the header of the manifest explains the mark.}}
}

\p{\strong{What a run says.} The executable of the patched build prints, for the default engine:}

\pre{\verb~|
default: engine machine (switches ...), policy practical, verify none (a rejection of the machine is final;
         -X verify=reference: re-checked by the reference engine), fallback reference (the reference engine
         decides a declaration the machine declines; -X fallback=none: the machine alone); one worker per CPU, ...
rlvm: stats: outcome=accept engine=machine reference=reference policy=practical verify=none fallback=reference workers=8(auto) ...
~}

\table{
\tr{\th{Command}\th{Rejection of the machine}\th{Unit the machine declines}\th{Rejected file with metadata}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm FILE~}, also \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck~}, \code{\verb~|-X verify=none~}, \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~}}\td{final}\td{decided by the reference engine}\td{rejected}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm -X verify=reference FILE~} (what a plain run of the published build does)}\td{re-checked: strict machine, then the reference engine}\td{decided by the reference engine}\td{checked again under the strict policy}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm -X fallback=none FILE~}, also \code{\verb~|-X no-fallback~}: the machine alone}\td{final}\td{declined}\td{rejected}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm -X verify=reference -X fallback=none FILE~}: the gate of an engine under test, as before}\td{confirmed, or declined as a disagreement}\td{declined}\td{checked again under the strict policy}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm -X engine=reference FILE~}, \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~}, \code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~}}\td{unchanged}\td{unchanged}\td{unchanged}}
}

\p{\strong{What is still the reference engine's in a plain run}, after the patch: the units the machine declines (section 2), and the environment pass (section 2.4).}

\subtree{
\title{7.1 Decisions inside the patch that are not in the instruction}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The runs of the reference engine keep the verification.} "The strict policy and \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~} and \code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~} are untouched" was a condition of the work. A rejection of the reference engine as primary engine is confirmed by one fresh cold run of itself, which is that engine's own protocol and not a second engine's opinion; under the practical policy such a run also keeps the net, for which it was built. So the default of \code{verify} is by engine: \code{none} for the machine and any engine that is not the reference engine, \code{reference} where the reference engine is the primary engine or the oracle. The mutant gate of the reference engine therefore keeps its counts, 15,741 and 2,034. If the maintainer wants the net off there too, it is one condition in \code{\verb~|MainOptions.resolveVerify~}.}
\li{\strong{\code{no-recheck} now means no net either.} In the published build \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck~} turns the driver's re-check off and leaves the launcher's net on. As the synonym of the new default it turns both off. A script that passes it gets a rejection instead of the net's verdict on a rejected file with metadata, and nothing else changes for it.}
\li{\strong{The driver's default changes with the launcher's}, so that a tool that drives the driver in process gets the production protocol unless it asks. The driver's own suites, the invariance script and the driver's bench ask.}
\li{\strong{"The machine alone" is not a new mechanism.} The driver had \code{no-fallback} for the gate of an engine under test; with a rejection that is final it means the primary engine alone. The patch gives it the value form \code{\verb~|fallback=reference|none~} beside \code{\verb~|verify=none|reference~} and makes the statistics say both. The environment pass stays the reference engine's under it.}
\li{\strong{No re-check by the strict machine alone.} It would repair 8 of the 12 wrong rejects without the reference engine and cost nothing on an accepted file. It was not built: the instruction as read is "a rejection of the primary engine is final; no strict re-run", and a fresh run that changes a verdict is exactly the thing that hides a dependence on the schedule (section 5.4).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7.2 What means the old behaviour and how it asks for it}

\table{
\tr{\th{Tool}\th{What it needs}\th{In the patch}}
\tr{\td{\src{scripts/gate.sh} with \code{\verb~|--defaults~}}\td{It gates what a user runs, so it gates the new default: the hostile step counts the known wrong rejects under their mark against the ceiling; the mutant step has 351 fewer declines. A gate of the verified configuration is \code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --defaults -X verify=reference~}, which gives the counts of before. Without \code{\verb~|--defaults~} the gate runs the reference engine and is unchanged.}\td{yes: the header, the constant, the option \code{\verb~|--known-wrong-rejects~}}}
\tr{\td{\src{scripts/gate-hostile.py}}\td{The mark \code{wrong-reject}, the ceiling, a line per known wrong reject and per marked row that is no longer rejected.}\td{yes}}
\tr{\td{The suites of \code{rlvm-driver}: the protocol table, the invariance suite on scripted engines, the runs on the arena tests}\td{The verification by name: the fixture sets it; the suite on the arena tests, where the reference engine is the primary engine, sets it as the launcher does.}\td{yes}}
\tr{\td{\src{rlvm-driver/bin/invariance.sh} and the driver's bench behind it}\td{\code{\verb~|verify=reference~} on every run unless the caller names the protocol; the bench keeps the verification for the reference engine as primary engine, as the launcher does.}\td{yes}}
\tr{\td{The gate of the machine, package M8: the pipeline step and the mutant campaign (\code{M8Pipeline.machine})}\td{\code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}: both read \code{driver.machineIncomplete} and \code{driver.warmAnomalies}, which only the re-check fills, and the campaign compares the final verdict with the reference pipeline's, which has the net.}\td{yes, one line; it must be applied together with the patch of \code{rlvm-main}, which knows the option}}
\tr{\td{The differential tools: \code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~} of the test kit, the driver's differential mode, \code{\verb~|testkit diff~}, the self-test of the gate}\td{Nothing: a comparison of engines verifies by the rule of section 7.1.}\td{no change}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|testkit judge~}, \src{scripts/check-light.sh}, \src{scripts/slice.sh}}\td{Nothing: they run what the gate tells them.}\td{no change}}
\tr{\td{The benchmark tools: \src{bench/warm.py}, the tables of [the benchmarks](rep-benchmarks), the arena run line}\td{Nothing, and the rows stay comparable across the switch-off: on a file that is accepted the re-check never ran, and the machine rejects no unit of the eight corpora.}\td{no change}}
\tr{\td{The in-process hostile suite of the machine (\code{SHostile})}\td{Nothing to run differently: it judges the machine's own answers. Its word "repaired by the re-check" for the rows with the note \code{recheck} now describes \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}; the rows are known wrong rejects of a plain run.}\td{only the manifest}}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. The gates of the patch}

\p{The patched build is a development distribution, executable and JVM launcher, built whole from a private copy of the main tree with the five diffs applied (jars sha256 \code{3babbe8de8f3}). Its jars of \code{rlvm-machine}, \code{rlvm-ref}, \code{rlvm-load}, \code{rlvm-store}, \code{rlvm-export}, \code{rlvm-level}, \code{rlvm-nat}, \code{rlvm-admit}, \code{rlvm-api} and \code{rlvm-cli} are byte for byte the published ones; \code{rlvm-driver} and \code{rlvm-main} differ by the patch; \code{rlvm-inductive} differs in its embedded build file only; \code{rlvm-truffle} has the classes of the main tree where the published jar is older, a difference that exists without the patch and concerns a tier that is off by default.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Gate}\th{Result}}
\tr{\td{Suites, in the private copy}\td{\code{rlvm-driver} 120 tests, 3 skipped, 0 failures (116 before; the 4 new ones are of section 7). \code{rlvm-main} 69 tests, 3 skipped, 0 failures (5 new). \code{rlvm-machine}: fast group 424, default group 455, and the slow hostile matrix, 56 switch sets by 1,274 rows, with the marked manifest: 0 failures.}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --defaults --corpus all --hostile --native~} of the patched tree on the patched build: the new default}\td{230 of 230 published tests correct, none declined. 17,775 mutants: SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 16,092, declined 1,683 (tutorial 11,847 and 413, bugs 2,627 and 65, prelude 839 and 8, metadata 779 and 1,197). The differential self-test caught. Hostile suite: agree 1,184, tolerated 74, known wrong rejects 12 of 12 marked and at most 12 allowed, wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 4. PASS.}}
\tr{\td{The same with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}: the old default by name}\td{230 of 230. Mutants: SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 15,741, declined 2,034. Hostile: agree 1,192, tolerated 78, known wrong rejects 0, the 12 marked rows listed as no longer rejected, declined 4. PASS. These are the counts of the published build.}}
\tr{\td{The new default through the jars on the JVM}\td{The same command without \code{\verb~|--native~}: 230 of 230; mutants SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 16,092, declined 1,683, no error and no timeout; the differential self-test caught; hostile agree 1,184, tolerated 74, known wrong rejects 12, wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 4. PASS.}}
\tr{\td{The eight corpora, 8 and 4 workers, executable and JVM launcher, no option}\td{32 of 32 runs accept. Each statistics line says \code{\verb~|verify=none fallback=reference~}; the reference engine decides 0, 1, 1, 0, 2, 16, 11 and 64 units in every run, as in the published build. Largest peak resident set 4.9 GB (Mathlib, JVM launcher, 8 workers).}}
\tr{\td{Every file of the census again, on the patched build, under \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}, no option, and \code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~}}\td{19,319 files by 3 configurations: 0 verdicts differ from what the published build gave under its default, under \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck~} and under \code{\verb~|-X no-recheck -X no-fallback~} with the net discounted. In the 14,238 verified runs with one worker every counter of the machine, of the reference engine and of the driver that was compared is identical; runs with more workers differ in who checks a repeat, as two runs of one build do.}}
\tr{\td{The runs of the reference engine, patched build against published build, file by file: the hostile suite, the metadata mutants and the 262 small arena tests, 3,512 files}\td{\code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~}: 0 verdicts differ; the net fires on the same files in both builds, the 4 files of finding R2-F4 and 358 mutants. \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~}: 0 verdicts differ. \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference -X no-recheck~}: 251 verdicts differ, the 4 files and 247 mutants, from a decline to a rejection. That is the one change of meaning of an existing option: \code{no-recheck} used to leave the net on and now is \code{\verb~|verify=none~}, which has none.}}
\tr{\td{The pipeline step of the machine's gate in the patched tree, default switch set (the one-line change of the fourth diff)}\td{The 175 tutorial and bugs tests: 175 verdicts right, the machine rejects 31 units, MACHINE-INCOMPLETE 0, WARM-ANOMALY 0. On the hostile row \code{\verb~|s3-wc-proj-two-decls-ok~} alone the step gives the right verdict and counts one WARM-ANOMALY, so the verification is in force in that harness. The complete gate of the machine and its mutant campaign were not run.}}
\tr{\td{The driver's invariance suite on real files, machine as primary engine}\td{section 5.4: with the re-check 12 hostile rows with a schedule difference, without it 21; 0 on the 17,159 files of the published tests and of the mutant corpora tutorial, bugs and metadata.}}
}

\p{\strong{The exact verdict changes of the default configuration}, patched against published, over all 19,319 files: 12 hostile rows from accept to reject, the wrong rejects of section 5.2; 4 hostile rows from accept to reject, the official verdict, section 5.3; 351 mutants from decline to reject, the official verdict, section 4. Nothing else. The eight corpora, the 148 good and 92 bad tests of the arena and its 30 tests of the class \code{either} are unchanged.}

\p{\strong{How the gate records the known wrong rejects.} A mark in the manifest, \code{wrong-reject}, like the mark of the tolerated kind but for the other direction, with the cause in the text of the row. \src{scripts/gate-hostile.py} judges a rejection of an official accept on a marked row as "known wrong reject" and on any other row as a wrong reject, which fails the gate as before. It prints every known wrong reject by name on every run and \src{scripts/gate.sh} repeats the lines, so the count is never only a number. The number may only go down: \src{scripts/gate.sh} carries it as a constant, 12, and passes it as a ceiling, so a new wrong reject is not admitted by a mark alone; a marked row that gets the official verdict is listed as "no longer rejected", and then the mark goes and the constant with it. The summary line reads: \code{\verb~|hostile: 1274 rows: agree 1184, kind-T accepts tolerated 74, KNOWN WRONG REJECT 12 (12 marked, at most 12), WRONG ACCEPT 0, WRONG REJECT 0, declined 4, errors 0~}.}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. What the READMEs and the contract notes must say after the switch-off}

\p{Nothing below was edited; the patch touches no README and no tree but this one. The sentences are given as they should read once the patch is applied. The counts are those of section 8.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Where}\th{Says today}\th{Must say}}
\tr{\td{\src{README.md}, the two engines}\td{the reference engine "confirms every rejection, decides every declaration the machine declines, and alone it is the strict mode"}\td{the reference engine admits the inductive types and the quotient package, decides every declaration the machine declines, and alone it is the strict mode; with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}, a development option, it re-checks every rejection of the machine.}}
\tr{\td{\src{README.md}, "A plain run"}\td{"the reference engine re-checks every rejection and decides what the machine declines"}\td{A plain run checks with the machine; a rejection of the machine is the verdict, and the reference engine decides only what the machine declines. \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~} prints the engine, its switch set and what verifies.}}
\tr{\td{\src{README.md}, exit status 1}\td{"a rejection is reported only after the reference engine has confirmed it"}\td{rejected: by the machine itself; with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} only after the reference engine has confirmed it.}}
\tr{\td{\src{README.md}, the option table}\td{no row}\td{\code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}: development; every rejection is re-checked by the reference engine and a rejected file with metadata is checked again under the strict policy (the default before the switch-off). \code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~}: the machine alone; a declaration it declines is declined.}}
\tr{\td{\src{README.md}, item (c) of the promise}\td{"everything the official checker accepts is accepted (a rejection is confirmed by the reference engine, a decline goes to it)"}\td{everything the official checker accepts is accepted on real input, and a declaration the machine declines goes to the reference engine. On hand-made hostile exports the machine is not aligned with the official checker yet: 12 exports that it accepts are rejected by a plain run; they are listed in the journal, counted by the gate, and \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} gives the official accept or a decline.}}
\tr{\td{\src{README.md}, "What stands behind this today", and the header of \src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml}}\td{mutants "agree 15,741, declined 2,034"; hostile "no wrong accept, no wrong reject; agree 1,124, 68 accepts of kind (d), 4 declined" (stale since the suite has 1,274 rows: 1,192, 78, 4)}\td{the counts of section 8: mutants agree 16,092, declined 1,683; hostile 1,274 rows, agree 1,184, 74 accepts of kind (d), 12 known wrong rejects, 4 declined.}}
\tr{\td{\src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml}, header and description}\td{"The reference engine ... re-checks every rejection before it is reported and decides every declaration the machine declines"; "so a rejection is never the machine's word alone"}\td{The reference engine decides every declaration the machine declines and admits the inductive types; a rejection is the machine's own. The second sentence goes, and so does "A rejection of a file in which the loader dropped a metadata node is checked again in the strict mode before it is reported".}}
\tr{\td{\src{rlvm-main/README.md}, the table of settings}\td{row \code{engine=NAME}: "the reference engine re-checks every rejection and decides what the machine declines"; row "\code{no-recheck}, \code{no-truffle}, \code{no-proof-blocks}: flags, off"; the driver's settings without \code{fallback}}\td{a row \code{\verb~|verify=none|reference~} with the rule of section 7 and its default by engine; \code{no-recheck} = \code{\verb~|verify=none~}; \code{\verb~|fallback=reference|none~} among the driver's settings; the row of \code{engine}: the reference engine decides what the machine declines.}}
\tr{\td{\src{rlvm-main/README.md}, "Policy", item (c) and "The safety net of the practical policy"}\td{"a rejection is always confirmed by the reference engine"; the net described as unconditional}\td{item (c) as in the root README; the net is part of \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} and of the runs whose primary engine is the reference engine; a plain run reports its rejection. The statistics line carries \code{verify=} and \code{fallback=}.}}
\tr{\td{\src{rlvm-driver/README.md}, "Re-check protocol" and "Verdict"}\td{"\code{\verb~|recheckRejections = false~} makes a REJECT of the primary engine final (measurement only)"; "A rejection is only ever recorded when confirmed"; "Rejections are still confirmed" under \code{no-fallback}}\td{\code{\verb~|recheckRejections = false~} is the default; the table of a warm REJECT is the development protocol (\code{\verb~|verify=reference~}); a rejection is recorded as the primary engine gave it, or after confirmation with the verification; \code{\verb~|fallback=none~} without the verification is the primary engine alone. The settings \code{verify} and \code{fallback} in the table of settings by name. The invariance suite asks for the verification by name.}}
\tr{\td{\src{rlvm-machine/README.md}}\td{"the reference engine re-checks every rejection and decides what the machine declines" (three places); "a kernel error is REJECT (confirmed by the driver with the reference kernel)"}\td{a kernel error is REJECT, and the driver reports it; with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} the driver confirms it first. The gate of the machine runs with that option.}}
\tr{\td{[the verdict protocol](design-verdict-protocol)}\td{status 1 is "a rejection confirmed by the reference engine"; "A rejection is only ever recorded when it is confirmed"; the re-check table as the protocol of every run; "it is what gives item (c)"}\td{status 1 is a rejection of the primary engine, of the environment pass or of a structural check. The re-check table is the protocol of \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} and of the runs of the reference engine; the rows of a warm decline and of an exception are the decline fallback, which stays in every run until the machine decides those units. New: without the re-check a rejection that comes out of a warm session depends on what the session checked before; on such input the verdict depends on the schedule (section 5.4).}}
\tr{\td{[the driver](design-driver), invariants}\td{"The verdict and the deciding unit do not depend on the worker count, the chunk and slice sizes, the session cap ..."}\td{that holds with the verification, and without it for every input on which no rejection of the primary engine depends on its session; the hostile rows of section 5.4 are the known exceptions.}}
\tr{\td{[the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract), item (c), the row "Loader" of the policy table, "the strict engine of the facade"}\td{"A rejection of the primary engine is always confirmed by the reference engine"; "A rejection of a file in which a node was dropped is not reported"; "the driver creates a second machine engine for the cold re-check of a rejection"}\td{item (c) holds on real input by the gates; on hostile input it has the 12 known exceptions of the default configuration, to be closed by aligning the machine, not by the reference engine. The strict re-run and the strict machine engine exist with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}.}}
\tr{\td{[the reference engine](design-reference-engine)}\td{its role as the engine that confirms rejections}\td{three roles in a plain run: the environment pass, the decline fallback, nothing else; the re-check is a development option.}}
\tr{\td{[the machine as default engine](dec-T79V)}\td{"As primary engine the machine has every rejection re-checked and every decline decided by the reference engine"; among the conditions of step 4: "the strict re-check still deciding every rejection of the machine"}\td{a note that this condition of the adversarial pass no longer holds for a plain run since the switch-off, by the maintainer's instruction, and that the pass was made with it.}}
\tr{\td{[the metadata class](dec-7UAL)}\td{status implemented; "The default configuration has no known rejection of an official accept"}\td{the strict re-run is off by default since the switch-off; the four files of the finding are rejected by a plain run; the 351 correct rejections are rejections again.}}
\tr{\td{[the gate](design-gate) and [the divergence classes](design-official-divergence)}\td{"no wrong accept and no wrong reject"; "which the pipeline's re-check repairs"; "It is closed" for the metadata class}\td{the hostile step has a third count, the known wrong rejects, with a ceiling that may only go down; the wrong rejects of classes 2, 3 and 5 are repaired only with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~}.}}
\tr{\td{[standing policies](standing-policies-exactness)}\td{"a rejection is always confirmed by the reference engine, and a decline goes to it"; "Never a rejection of what the official checker accepts ... also on a hand-made file"}\td{the maintainer's instruction of 2026-11-25, verbatim, with its consequence: the second sentence is the goal for the machine itself and has 12 known exceptions on hand-made files until it is reached.}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10. Not established}

\ul{
\li{\strong{x86-64.} Everything ran on the aarch64 development host. No timing was taken: the patch changes what happens after a rejection, which no corpus has, and the machine's jar is the published one byte for byte.}
\li{\strong{Whether the list of twelve is complete.} It is complete for the 1,274 rows of the suite. The two causes are classes, and other hand-made files of the same classes will be rejected too; the suite was not extended.}
\li{\strong{The environment pass with the machine.} Section 2.4 counts the units; nothing was built or compared.}
\li{\strong{The cost of aligning}, section 5.3, is the journal's measurements of what each mechanism gains, taken with earlier switch sets and builds. No aligned variant was built or timed. For warm sessions that keep values only, for the official caches and for the official type of an unreduced term there is no number at all.}
\li{\strong{The two mutants declined for memory} were not analysed: why the machine needs more than the memory limit where the reference engine rejects within it.}
\li{\strong{The arena's own procedure} (the 270 tests in the arena's container) was not repeated on the patched build; the 262 small tests and the eight corpora were run through the executable outside it.}
\li{\strong{The invariance suite} was run with the machine as primary engine only, and not on the prelude mutants or the large corpora.}
\li{\strong{The standing policy.} "Never a rejection of what the official checker accepts, also on a hand-made file" and the maintainer's instruction of 2026-11-25 cannot both hold until the machine is aligned. The report takes the instruction as the later word and records the twelve; the policy tree was not edited.}
}
}
